[Music]  this is a medical image of a brain tumor  most cancer patients undergoing brain  surgery don't even know what kind of  tumor they have  because getting a diagnosis can take a  while a doctor has to take a tissue  sample  and send it to a lab where it's then  examined  prepared as a slide and interpreted by a  specialist to get a diagnosis  but now artificial intelligence is  rapidly speeding up this process  using millions of medical images  researchers have trained an algorithm to  predict an accurate cancer diagnosis  within minutes not only can this improve  the speed and accuracy of surgery  but it can streamline the traditional  workflow for getting a diagnosis  especially for hospitals that don't have  easy access to a lab  this is just one example of how people  are using artificial intelligence today  ai has expanded well beyond the field of  computer science  into many other parts of society so what  else are we using it for  how have we integrated ai into our  personal  and professional lives  [Music]  ai research has come a long way we're at  the point now where systems can even do  some things better than humans  we're using ai and everything from  hiring practices  to online banking to how we choose music  ai has a lot of presence in people's  lives it's in  a lot of the devices that we use every  single day when you  use your smartphone for instance these  are all examples of ai applications  when i use google maps google maps uses  ai and  when i use youtube youtube makes  recommendations it's the way  that siri can listen to your voice and  search the internet for what you're  looking for  recommending movies if you use netflix  or recommending  products if you use amazon  but for an algorithm to function really  well its task has to be specific  and very well defined that's the tricky  part  people kind of expect ai to be perfect  all the time or to not really make  mistakes  again this kind of goes back to a more  traditional view of what ai was like  something that acted  or thought kind of like a human thinks  and acts that's not really the case at  all  let's just take one of the examples that  you know  we're all very familiar with right now  which is  digital assistance so you come home and  you say alexa play some  christmas music it will pull up  something based upon  some basic training that it's had to  associate certain words with certain  content  but the other day when i was at my  friend's house and  he said you know alexa please put on  some fun music and it was totally  confused i didn't even know what to do  with that because it's expecting a genre  right  the instant you do something kind of  weird or not what it's  been trained on it completely falls  apart it doesn't even come close to  providing you with what you want  okay so ai may still be flawed but it's  really useful  it can help us process and analyze huge  amounts of data  which is why we've come to rely on it in  everyday life  it has the potential to transform every  industry  one of the biggest examples is  healthcare  i focus on machine learning for health i  try to train machine learning algorithms  to  do what a clinician might have done or  inform a clinician about what processes  might be better for them  maybe a doctor wants to know every six  hours whether a patient might need an  intervention  if we can give them that information  with high accuracy it can help them plan  which patients they can focus on  we're also using ai in other areas like  immigration  people have started to look at  artificial intelligence as a means of  analyzing these big mountains of data  to help people for example with refugee  claims  if you know we don't have the human  capacity to go through all of this data  but maybe ai can quickly find these are  the people who are most at risk or  in need we should think of ai as a tool  it does come with some challenges but  it's a powerful technology  that has immense potential to improve  human life  as we look towards the future we just  have to make sure that we're using it in  a way that benefits  all of us if people aren't informed and  are worried about these hypothetical  situations it can  distract or devolve the conversation for  instance killer robots  aren't going to show up tomorrow but  bias and privacy are  very important issues that are affecting  people today it's like a paintbrush  we could paint some really ugly things  but we could also paint really beautiful  things  and it's up to us as a society to decide  that these tools machine learning and  artificial intelligence  are tools that we want to use for public  good and we're going to enable them to  do that  and regulate them to do only that  [Music]

